Hi Komal, thanks for your email, through which you have requested to provide a copy of both orders passed by the Supreme Court of Pakistan (SCP) and Sindh High Court (SHC), respectively, on chargeability of Sindh Sales Tax (SST) on distributor and their impact Shell Pakistan Limited transactions in the case of lubricant sales to distributors.
At the outset, it is important to understand the background SHC order, through its judgment dated September 6, 2021, dismissed the appeal filed by the distributor, M/s M. Mubashir Traders (MT), against the SRB Tribunal’s decision dated November 22, 2018. SHC determined that MT's activities constituted distribution services rather than the sale of goods, thereby subjecting MT's revenue to Sindh Sales Tax (SST) instead of Federal Sales Tax (FST). The conclusions drawn by SHC (and previously by the SRB Tribunal) were based on the contractual arrangements between MT and Colgate Palmolive (CP), which stipulated:
(i) Manufacturer appointed the distributor for specific territories/areas;
(ii) Distributor was responsible for product promotion and sales enhancement;
(iii) Distributor was obligated to maintain adequate stock levels;
(iv) Distributor was required to submit periodic stock reports to the manufacturer;
(v) Manufacturer retained the right to repurchase unsold stock from the distributor;
(vi) Distributor was bound by the manufacturer's directives regarding sales, pricing, and sales territories;
(vii) Distributor facilitated the manufacturer's sales efforts; and
(viii) The agreement between manufacturer and distributor delineated the nature of services.

SCP's decision upholds SHC's findings, which were primarily based on the contractual elements between CP and MT. Consequently, transactions between manufacturers and distributors are construed as service arrangements, subjecting them to SST under the category of 'supply chain management or distribution (including delivery) services' [tariff heading 9845.0000]. Thus, FST does not apply as these transactions are not treated as 'sale of goods'.
The question arises: Does SCP's decision mean that all distribution agreements are now considered service arrangements?

In this regard, we believe that SCP's decision cannot be uniformly applied to all distribution arrangements. Its implications should be assessed on a case-by-case basis and limited to contracts/arrangements where the transfer of risk and rewards (i.e., title to goods) is conditional, with the manufacturer retaining control over pricing, discounts, etc. Transactions where risk and rewards are transferred without manufacturer control over the goods can still be argued to be subject to FST (rather than SST).
Previous disputes over the determination of 'sale of goods' versus services/agency relationships have been litigated. SCP's decision in the case of M/s Pak Saudi Fertilizers Limited (2023 SCMR 1595) dated March 13, 2023, ruled that a transaction constituted an outright sale based on the criteria outlined in the Sale of Goods Act, 1930. Similar disputes were resolved by SHC in the case of M/s Brook Bond Pakistan Limited (No. ITC No.300/2004) dated October 22, 2020, where substance prevailed over form in determining the nature of the transaction, which was treated as a sale of goods based on the Sale of Goods Act, 1930. 

Additionally, SCP's decision (CP No.243/2011 & CP No.1136/2018) dated May 31, 2022, in the case of M/s. Pakistan Tobacco Company Limited & Khurshid Ali (Pepsi Cola Dealer) underscored that the same definition of 'sale' underlies both the Sale of Goods Act, 1930, and the Sales Tax Act, 1990. Thus, transactions where risk and rewards are outrightly transferred from seller to buyer are considered sales under both Acts. 
SCP's recent decision (January 26, 2024) should be applied within the context of distributor agreements akin to CP and MT's arrangement. It should not be construed as universally applicable to all distribution agreements but rather contingent upon specific contractual facts.
It is advisable to critically review distribution agreements to ascertain whether the transfer of risk and rewards in goods is effectively being accomplished. If agreements satisfy the criteria outlined in the Sale of Goods Act, 1930, then FST applies. Conversely, if the terms indicate that goods have not been sold (due to manufacturer control), provincial tax implications may arise.

